To completely finish this, you just need to make sure your **VoucherQueryController.java** is also catching those specific exceptions.
Open your **VoucherQueryController.java** and replace your downloadBatchExcel method with this one. Notice how we change the return type to ResponseEntity<?> and add the specific catch block for InvalidDataException:
```java
    @GetMapping("/download-batch/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<?> downloadBatchExcel(@PathVariable String batchId, @RequestHeader("Authorization") String token) {
        try {
            String userId = jwtUtil.getUserIdFromToken(token.substring(7));
            Integer roleId = jwtUtil.getUserRoleFromToken(token.substring(7));
            
            if (!queryService.isUserAuthorizedForBatch(batchId, userId, roleId)) {
                return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN)
                        .body(Map.of("message", "You are not authorized to view this batch."));
            }
            
            byte[] fileData = queryService.generateBatchExcel(batchId);
            
            org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders headers = new org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders();
            headers.setCacheControl(org.springframework.http.CacheControl.noCache().mustRevalidate().sMaxAge(java.time.Duration.ZERO)); 
            headers.set(org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders.CONTENT_DISPOSITION, "attachment; filename=\"Batch_" + batchId + ".xlsx\""); 
            headers.set(org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders.CONTENT_TYPE, "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet");
            
            // Returns the Excel file if successful
            return ResponseEntity.ok().headers(headers).body(fileData);
            
        } catch (CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException e) {
            // ✨ THE CRITICAL CHANGE: Catches the service error ("No records found") and sends it to React
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST).body(Map.of("message", e.getMessage()));
            
        } catch (Exception e) { 
            log.error("DOWNLOAD_ERROR | Batch: {} | Reason: {}", batchId, e.getMessage());
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
                    .body(Map.of("message", "Failed to generate Excel file."));
        }
    }

```
### Summary of the Strategy We Just Applied:
 1. **Your Service** checks rules (e.g., "Are there rows in this batch?") and throws an InvalidDataException if the rule is broken.
 2. **Your Controller** has a specific catch (CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException e) block waiting for it.
 3. **Your Controller** safely wraps e.getMessage() inside a 400 Bad Request and sends it to React.
 4. **Your React Frontend** reads err.response.data.message and displays it in the red Snackbar.
As long as you use the VoucherActionController.java I provided earlier, and update this single endpoint in your VoucherQueryController.java, your entire application is perfectly wired up to show custom error messages!
